; font.tal - the character set, drawn a pixel at a time.
;
;   ./duxasm -X -o roms/font.rom asm/font.tal
;   ./dux -n 2 -p /tmp/font.ppm roms/font.rom
;   ./duxsdl roms/font.rom
;
; A specimen sheet: every character the machine can draw, once, in the order it
; makes sense to read them rather than in one run. Seven rows, because printable
; ASCII is ninety five characters and the widest run of them - the twenty six
; letters - does not fit across three hundred and twenty pixels at a pitch that
; still leaves the glyphs apart.
;
;     0123456789          the digits
;     ABC...Z             upper case
;     abc...z             lower case
;      !"#$%&'()*+,-./   punctuation
;     :;<=>?@             more punctuation
;     [\]^_`              brackets and the two odd ones
;     {|}~                braces
;
; What is on screen
; -----------------
; The glyphs are the printable ASCII of the 512_8 font, in asm/lib/ascii.tal,
; generated by tools/font512.py from https://github.com/alexfru/512_8. That is
; public domain (Unlicense), so nothing is owed for using it, and it replaced a
; hand-built table of forty one glyphs. Ninety five is 760 bytes where the whole
; five hundred and twelve glyph font is 4096.
;
; One thing to know before trusting this sheet as a reference: there is NO plain
; exclamation mark in 512_8. The author put a double one at $21 in its place, so
; the second character on the punctuation row is two marks, not one. It is not a
; mistake here and it is not going to be fixed here - both styles of the font
; carry the same characters at the same indices, so it would be a change to the
; font, not to this program.
;
; Why a bitmap and not seven segments
; ----------------------------------
; A seven-segment figure cannot be a right-shaped digit: the 1 has no foot, the 4
; is open-topped, the 7 is bare, and no choice of masks or segment offsets
; changes that. The shape is not in the data, it is in the seven pieces. So every
; glyph here is a literal picture: eight rows of eight pixels, one byte a row, bit
; 7 the leftmost pixel. What you see is what the table says, pixel for pixel, and
; changing a glyph means changing one byte.
;
; How it is drawn
; --------------
; One @plot per lit pixel. Not the sprite port, which packs four pixels to a byte
; at two bits each (measured, wip/bitprobe.tal) and has to be re-decoded every time
; something goes wrong in it. @plot is the one drawing path on this machine with
; no interpretation in it.
;
; The eight columns of a row are unrolled with their masks written in - $80, $40,
; $20 ... $01 - rather than walked round an eight-byte table. A mask table needs
; its address computed, and every way of computing that address on this machine has
; been wrong at least once: a byte loop that indexes one byte per column and a
; pointer left at the wrong place both look like a font with holes in it.
;
; @glyph hands back the pointer to the NEXT glyph rather than taking an index, so
; laying out a row of them is a walk with no arithmetic in it. Every glyph is
; eight bytes, so "next" is "eight further on" and the caller just keeps what it
; was given. There is no multiply anywhere in this file.
;
; A row is a CONTIGUOUS run of characters, which is why each one has its own start
; pointer: the table is in character order from $20, so @20, @41 and @5b name the
; first glyph of a run and @glyph walks the rest. The old table was in one piece
; - digits, then letters, then marks - so it could start at @font and walk the lot.
; This one cannot, because 0x39 is a digit and 0x41 is an A with a colon and two
; others between them.
;
; Stack contracts, all checked by the assembler and tools/netcheck.py:
;   @glyph  x short, y short, glyph short, colour byte   seven in, two out
;   @line   ptr short, y short, count byte, colour byte  eight in, none out
INCLUDE "common.tal"
INCLUDE "lib/fontlib.tal"
INCLUDE "lib/ascii.tal"

; --------------------------------------------------------------- zero page
;
; Private block per routine, and the blocks do not touch: @glyph calls @plot,
; which is common.tal's and owns $10 to $26, so everything here starts at $80.
@ZP.lx    = $8a                    ; @line's walk. The glyph's own cells belong
@ZP.ly    = $8c                    ; to fontlib, which owns $a0 upwards.
@ZP.ln    = $8e
@ZP.lc    = $8f
@ZP.fp    = $a4                    ; @line reuses fontlib's pointer cell for its
                                  ; own walk and is the only thing that touches
                                  ; it between a @glyph call and the next

; ------------------------------------------------------------------ layout
;
; A pitch of eleven is eight of glyph and three of space. Twelve does not fit:
; the letter rows are twenty six glyphs, so twenty at x=20 would run to 328 and
; off the right edge. Eleven puts the last letter's right edge at 291.
@X0    = $08                       ; 8, the left margin
@X.COL2= $a0                       ; 160, the second column
@X.COL3= $64                       ; 100
@X.COL4= $b4                       ; 180
@PITCH = $0b                       ; 11: eight of glyph and three of space
@R.Y0  = $0e                       ; 14, the top row. Five rows at eighteen apart
                                  ; end at 86, well clear of the bottom.

; The seven runs, and where each one starts. A glyph is at @font + (c - $20) * 8,
; so each of these is that product written out; @font + 128 and not @font + 16*8
; because a symbol followed by an operator only parses inside a definition.
@GL.DIGITS = @font + 128           ; $30, ten
@GL.UPPER = @font + 264            ; $41, thirteen
@GL.UPPER2= @font + 368            ; $4e, thirteen more
@GL.LOWER = @font + 520            ; $61, thirteen
@GL.LOWER2= @font + 624            ; $6e, thirteen more
@GL.SYM1  = @font + 0              ; $20, sixteen: space to solidus
@GL.SYM2  = @font + 208            ; $3a, seven: colon to commercial at
@GL.SYM3  = @font + 472            ; $5b, six: bracket to grave accent
@GL.SYM4  = @font + 728            ; $7b, four: brace to tilde

; Sixteen per line is the most that fits at eleven apart with a margin, and the
; letter rows are two runs of thirteen rather than one of twenty six because
; twenty six glyphs at a readable pitch is wider than the screen.
@draw
        ; The digits, $30 to $39.
        LIT2 @GL.DIGITS
        LIT2 @X0
        LIT2 @R.Y0
        LIT $0a
        LIT $01                    ; white
        JSR2 @line

        ; Upper case, $41 to $5a. Thirteen at a time, the second half beside the
        ; first rather than below it: thirteen glyphs end at column 148 and the
        ; second run starts at 160, which is 300 for its last glyph's right edge.
        LIT2 @GL.UPPER
        LIT2 @X0
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 18
        LIT $0d
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @line

        LIT2 @GL.UPPER2
        LIT2 @X.COL2
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 18
        LIT $0d
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @line

        ; Lower case, $61 to $7a, same two halves.
        LIT2 @GL.LOWER
        LIT2 @X0
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 36
        LIT $0d
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @line

        LIT2 @GL.LOWER2
        LIT2 @X.COL2
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 36
        LIT $0d
        LIT $01
        JSR2 @line

        ; Punctuation, in grey so it reads as the tail of the sheet rather than as
        ; more letters. The space at the front of the first row draws nothing,
        ; which is the point of drawing it.
        LIT2 @GL.SYM1
        LIT2 @X0
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 54
        LIT $10                    ; sixteen, $20 to $2f
        LIT $03
        JSR2 @line

        ; And the rest of it on one line: seven, then six, then four, with a gap
        ; between each so the three runs are not read as one long row. They are
        ; $3a to $40, $5b to $60 and $7b to $7e - the punctuation that is not in
        ; the first sixteen, plus the two brackets and the backtick.
        LIT2 @GL.SYM2
        LIT2 @X0
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 72
        LIT $07
        LIT $03
        JSR2 @line

        LIT2 @GL.SYM3
        LIT2 @X.COL3
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 72
        LIT $06
        LIT $03
        JSR2 @line

        LIT2 @GL.SYM4
        LIT2 @X.COL4
        LIT2 @R.Y0 + 72
        LIT $04
        LIT $03
        JSR2 @line

        JMP2r2

; Stack: ptr short, x short, y short, count byte, colour byte. Eight in, none out.
;
; count glyphs from the pointer, at that column, one pitch apart.
;
; The column is an argument rather than a constant because the letter rows are
; two runs side by side. A-M and N-Z are both thirteen glyphs and both want to
; start at the left margin, so whichever started second would land on top of the
; first and the row would read as thirteen letters and four overlapping
; accidents - which is exactly what it did.
@line
        STA $ZP.lc
        STA $ZP.ln
        STA2 $ZP.ly
        STA2 $ZP.lx
        STA2 $ZP.fp
@line-loop
        LDA2 $ZP.lx
        LDA2 $ZP.ly
        LDA2 $ZP.fp                ; where this glyph is
        LDZ $ZP.lc
        JSR2 @glyph
        STA2 $ZP.fp                ; and @glyph hands back where the next is
        LDA2 $ZP.lx
        LIT2 @PITCH
        ADD2
        STA2 $ZP.lx
        LDZ $ZP.ln
        LIT $01
        SUB
        DUP                         ; JNZ eats the flag, so keep a copy to store
        STA $ZP.ln
        JNZ @line-loop
        JMP2r2